Z||000000||Digisys||09-24-2001||07:52 PM||||I am having some valve troubles! (this is on a 1988 V6) I adjusted the valve lash by placing the No. 1 cylinder on TDC and then adjusting 1,5,6 intake and 1,2,3 exhaust (turn them 3/4 turn after feeling drag on the pushrod as I was turning them) I then rotated the crankshaft 360 degrees and adjusted 2,3,4 intake and 4,5,6, exhaust. I have just the bottom manifold on when I go to check compression I have none but I can feel air blowing thru the intake manifold. I know I am overlooking something here, I am beginning to wonder if I am identifing the valves correctly (exhaust and intake) when I am adjusting them. I have no doubts about the firing order, etc. Can anyone sketch me a diagram of the valve layout, I really doubt I have these confused (which is exhaust and which intake) but I can not find any other problems. PS. I have tried different adjustment for the lash from 1/2 turn to 1 1/2 turns. Any ideas ?||||reg||9
Z||000001||87GTZ34||09-25-2001||09:27 AM||||The recommended valve lash adjustment is to center the lifter plunger in its travel within the lifter.  It sounds like the piston was all the way from not having any tension on them and they haven't bled down.  When I rebuilt my heads, I used the recommended 1.5 turns to center the plunger and it worked perfectly.  I didn't take a comp test as the lifters are designed to adjust with the pressure.  Yours should do the same but I would use the 1.5 turns and not the 3/4 turns to give the lifters the most margin possible.  Now if you have after market rockers, you will need to calculate how many turns in order to center the lifter plunger.  The mfg of the rockers may give this info.  Someone had the same questions a while ago, and you may want to search that one to see what the outcome was.||||reg||1
